findings.problem:
The garage clutter palette passes the job yellow '#ffc629' as one of its five colours (garage.ts:31 `colors:['#c98f5a','#d7a56d','#2fb3a6','#ffc629','#9fb8c8']`). Every crates, cart and cabinets recipe can draw it. On top of that, the level props add a yellow chair at (-2.6, 5.2) (garage.ts:16), a yellow mail cart at (-1, 6.6) (garage.ts:19), a yellow reel at (-4.4, 6.8) (garage.ts:12), and the dolly's yellow-flanged reel at (9.3, 4.1).

In the start shot about 2.8% of the play-area pixels are job yellow (r>220, 170<g<215, b<90). These include:
- a yellow-drawered cabinet column directly behind the bench scope at (-1.6, -4.91), which fills the top-left of the bench view;
- a second yellow column at (-8.21, 3.21);
- yellow crate pairs at the right wall;
- two yellow mail carts next to spawn.

The actual job items (the PULSE button, the part tray and the robot's lid) use the same yellow, and so does the objective marker cone. A first-time viewer of the start shot cannot pick out the 'job thing' by colour. The grab prompt also offers yellow junk ('Grab the crate', 'Grab the splitter'). RESEARCH_ART F7 already noted that yellow job highlights lose contrast in the garage. This makes it worse.

The job robot (a cart prop, id 'robot', at (6.4, -5.2)) is drawn with robotMesh() in TEAL (station.ts:105). The decor robots at (4.4, -5.2) and (8.4, -5.2) use the same robotMesh, in TEAL and '#4aa3d8' (room.ts:87). All three:
- sit on identical glowing pads;
- have identical wall chargers;
- park nose-in toward the back wall.

The 4.4 m one is pixel-identical to the job robot (garage-models-10-robot-pad, -20-wheels-right, -22). Nothing on the model says 'faulty' or 'this one': no warning lamp, no open panel, no tag, no hazard stripe. A player who can't see the marker (it is a pale translucent cone, see the marker finding) has a one-in-three guess. The decor robots are solid colliders, so pressing E near them gives no prompt and no feedback. AGENTS 2 asks for 'play by doing': the world should show which robot is broken.

findings.fix:
1) Make the job robot visibly broken until it is fixed. In GarageBench.update, while !onStand:
   - blink the antenna lamp red (r.lamp.material = hot('#ff5a4d',2) at 1.5 Hz);
   - show '×' eyes (scale the eyes to a flat X, or swap the face material for a red-cross canvas);
   - emit a small spark every 2.5 s from the head (game.burst at root + (.3,.6,0), 'spark', 4);
   - hang a yellow/ink 'REPAIR' luggage tag on the antenna: rbox(.12,.08,.01) with a hazard texture, tied with a TubeGeometry string.
2) Recolour the decor robots so neither matches: for example '#6cc58a' and '#b392f0' bodies with CREAM cargo bins.
3) Turn the decor robots off: dim the eyes (toon('#34424a')) and show a full charge bar on their chargers, so they read as 'charged, parked, fine'.

findings.problem:
The research pass (research-external F4) flagged this. It is unchanged. When PULSE lands with no path, the arc uses:
- a core of hot('#e8f8ff',3) (station.ts:186);
- an additive glow sprite of size .7 at opacity .8 (station.ts:188), above the DR-66 0.5 cap;
- a PointLight on the board (station.ts:189);
- arcT=.6 s, plus spark bursts.

The crops at +0.0 s and +0.4 s after the switch opens (garage-models-70-arc-0.0, -72-arc-0.4) show the switch's black body, the NODE test point and the dashed ACROSS SWITCH socket bleached into one pale blob. The whole left half of the PCB is tinted white. The full frame at +1.25 s (-57) still shows the bloom. This is the exact moment and part being taught (no path, so the current arcs across the switch), and the effect hides the switch. It also exceeds DR-79's 120 ms flash limit near a taught part.

Only pillars are registered as fade occluders (decor.ts:55 game.occluders.push), plus meshes built with `fade` (game.ts:113). The fade ray goes to p.y+.4 only (game.ts:387-388). In the garage, none of the tall things fade:
- the clutter `cabinets` recipe builds two 1.6 m cabinets with a box on top, 2.2 m columns (clutter.ts:46), at (-1.6, -4.91) and (-8.21, 3.21) per the audit;
- the side cabinets at (10.2, -.6/.2);
- the tilted scope (1.46×.92 m, station.ts:169-170);
- the tool chest and the tyre stacks.

In garage-models-41-left-front-corner-o180 Pip at (-8.4, 5.2) is completely hidden behind the cabinet column; only the hard-hat crown shows. In garage-models-38-clutter-cab-behind-bench Pip, standing behind the bench at (-.6, -4.0) in the robot-carry corridor, is hidden behind the scope. In -52 the drawers column dominates. The second one sits right in the robot's carry path between the pad and the stand.

This re-check of RESEARCH_ART F7 / DR-54 found it still open. In garage-models-01-start the median floor luma is 218 (mid), 226 (left) and 214 (front). With ?norim the values are identical (garage-models-80-start-norim), so the rim light is not the cause. The rule is 140–185.

Causes:
- TX.concreteFloor('#dcd8ce') at room.ts:66;
- MOODS.garage key 2.15, hemi .74, exposure 1.02 (toon.ts:64);
- 7 point lights (room.ts:105-110).

In the zoom-9 shots the cream bench (#efe2c8), cream wall chargers, white parcel labels, pale grey-blue boxes (#9fb8c8) and the pale cabinets (#9fb8c8 props at x 10.2) all sit within about 20 luma of the floor. Contact shadows are faint, so props look pasted on (garage-models-20, -34, -35). The yellow job items sit on a white field, which is the worst case for yellow.

The garage's cable is hidden and not simulated (game.ts:104; station rooms). Yet the room is dressed with the game's job-item vocabulary:
- the level reel at (-4.4, 6.8) in job yellow (garage.ts:12);
- the cable dolly with its yellow-flanged reel at (9.3, 4.1) (garage.ts:12, prefab at prefabs.ts:160-162);
- the `parts` recipe, listed twice in the garage recipes (garage.ts:31), each copy adding a reel and a yellow '#ffc94d' splitter (clutter.ts:44).

Shots: a white-drum reel on the rug at about (-7.6, 3.6) and a yellow splitter beside it (garage-models-30, -40). The prompt even reads 'E Grab the splitter' (-40). In every cable level these exact objects are the things you pick up to solve the job. Here they are physics junk and teach the player to grab the wrong thing.

The box prefab has one fixed size .7×.6×.7 and identical dressing: one tape strip over the top and down two faces, and a white label with two ink lines at the same spot (prefabs.ts:56-62). Garage boxes take colours from the clutter palette, including '#2fb3a6' teal, '#ffc629' yellow and '#9fb8c8' grey-blue (garage.ts:31), and the level props add teal boxes at (8.6, 6.6) and (9.5, 6) (garage.ts:11).

The result, in garage-models-18-dock, -19, -20, -33, -34, -35:
- dozens of identical cubes in five colours;
- the grey-blue and teal ones read as plastic storage bins, not cardboard;
- rows of identical labels repeat in lockstep (e.g. the yellow/grey row in -33-desk-coworker-o180).

Boxes are the most common prop in the room's 111 (snap().props), so the repetition is the dominant texture of the room. This breaks the DR-51 cardboard-is-kraft rule and the DR-67 variety rule.

findings.fix:
1) Restrict box colours to kraft: '#c98f5a', '#d7a56d', '#b9875a', '#e0b47a' (see the yellow finding).
2) In prefabs.ts build('box'), use `variant` (already passed through clutter specs):
   - v%3 picks a size scale: 1 / (.8,.7,.8) / (1,.45,1.15), a flat parcel, with a matching prefab size via a `boxSizes` table;
   - v%4 picks a label position (front left, front right, top, none);
   - v%5===0 adds a red 'FRAGILE' stripe decal or ↑↑ arrows;
   - v%6===0 opens one top flap: a thin rbox rotated .6 rad.
3) Have the crates recipe in clutter.ts set variant: Math.floor(r()*30).

